/* Bassiet book, Depth 10: The Nephron Catacombs. Original text. */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
BOOK['c10-glom']={depth:10,title:'The Sieve',
epi:'A knot of capillaries pressed against a cup. Everything small passes through. Everything that should stay, stays. Unless the sieve tears.',
c:['nephritic-nephrotic','gfr','hypersensitivity','diabetes'],
S:[
{h:'Filtration',p:[
'The glomerular barrier has three layers: **fenestrated endothelium**, **basement membrane** (type IV collagen, negatively charged heparan sulfate), and **podocyte foot processes** with slit diaphragms (nephrin). It blocks large and negatively charged molecules such as albumin. Losing the charge barrier alone (minimal change disease) lets albumin through selectively; tearing the structure lets through bigger proteins and red cells.',
'**GFR** depends on Starling forces: GFR = Kf × [(P_GC − P_BS) − (π_GC − π_BS)]. Glomerular capillary pressure (P_GC) pushes fluid out; Bowman\'s space pressure (P_BS) and plasma oncotic pressure (π_GC, which rises along the capillary as protein-free fluid leaves) push back. The **afferent arteriole** controls inflow (prostaglandins keep it open, so NSAIDs narrow it); the **efferent arteriole** controls outflow (constricted by angiotensin II, dilated by ACE inhibitors). The **filtration fraction** (FF = GFR / RPF) is normally about 20%.',
'GFR stays steady across a wide range of blood pressures by **autoregulation**: the afferent arteriole contracts when stretched (myogenic response), and when the macula densa senses more NaCl it releases adenosine, which constricts the afferent arteriole (**tubuloglomerular feedback**). SGLT2 inhibitors work partly through this loop: more sodium reaches the macula densa, the afferent arteriole tightens, and glomerular pressure falls.'],
table:{cols:['Measure','Marker'],rows:[['GFR','inulin clearance (exact); creatinine clearance (slightly overestimates, a little is secreted)'],['Effective renal plasma flow','para-aminohippurate (PAH) clearance (filtered and secreted, so almost cleared in one pass)'],['Renal blood flow','RPF / (1 − haematocrit)'],['Clearance of any substance','C = (U × V) / P'],['Filtered load','GFR × plasma concentration']]},
s:[['pharm','NSAIDs (afferent constriction) plus ACE inhibitors (efferent dilation) plus diuretics ("triple whammy") can precipitate acute kidney injury, especially in the elderly or dehydrated.'],['phys','Compare a substance\'s clearance with GFR: equal means filtered only (inulin); lower means net reabsorption (glucose, urea, sodium); higher means net secretion (PAH, creatinine slightly).']]},
{h:'Arterioles, plasma flow and filtration fraction',p:[
'Think of the glomerulus as a tap and a drain. **Afferent** changes alter the flow coming in and the pressure inside together, so RPF and GFR move in the **same direction** and FF hardly changes. **Efferent** constriction slows the drain: less plasma flows through (RPF falls), but pressure backs up inside, so GFR rises (with moderate constriction) and FF rises more. Changes in the forces opposing filtration (plasma protein, Bowman\'s pressure) alter GFR without touching RPF.'],
table:{cols:['Change','RPF','GFR','FF'],rows:[
['Afferent constriction (NSAIDs, sympathetic tone)','↓','↓','no change'],
['Afferent dilation (prostaglandins)','↑','↑','no change'],
['Efferent constriction (angiotensin II)','↓','↑','↑'],
['Efferent dilation (ACE inhibitors, ARBs)','↑','↓','↓'],
['Raised plasma protein (myeloma, dehydration)','no change','↓','↓'],
['Low plasma protein (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis)','no change','↑','↑'],
['Ureteric obstruction (raised Bowman\'s pressure)','no change','↓','↓'],
['Volume depletion (sympathetic tone and angiotensin II)','↓↓','↓','↑']]},
key:'Afferent changes move RPF and GFR together; efferent changes pull them apart.'},
{h:'Nephrotic syndrome',p:[
'Damage to podocytes lets protein leak: **proteinuria above 3.5 g/day**, low albumin, oedema, hyperlipidaemia (the liver overproduces lipoproteins; fatty casts and oval fat bodies with "Maltese crosses" under polarised light), hypercoagulability (antithrombin lost in urine, clotting factors overmade: **renal vein thrombosis**, especially in membranous nephropathy), infection risk (immunoglobulins lost; spontaneous bacterial peritonitis in children).',
'Primary podocyte diseases (minimal change, FSGS) give a "bland" nephrotic picture; immune-complex and systemic diseases (membranous, lupus, diabetes, amyloid) can mix nephrotic and nephritic features. In children minimal change is so common that steroids are given without a biopsy; in adults the biopsy decides.'],
table:{cols:['Disease','Who','Biopsy'],rows:[
['Minimal change disease','children (commonest); Hodgkin lymphoma; NSAIDs','normal light microscopy, effaced foot processes on EM; responds to steroids'],
['Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis','adults; commonest in Black patients; HIV (collapsing variant), heroin, sickle cell, obesity, APOL1 variants','segmental sclerosis and hyalinosis; poor steroid response'],
['Membranous nephropathy','commonest primary cause in white adults; anti-PLA2R antibodies; secondary to SLE, hepatitis B/C, solid tumours, NSAIDs, penicillamine','thick basement membrane, "spike and dome" subepithelial deposits, granular IF'],
['Diabetic nephropathy','long-standing diabetes','Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules, mesangial expansion, thick basement membrane; ACE inhibitors or ARBs, SGLT2 inhibitors'],
['Amyloidosis','myeloma (AL), chronic inflammation (AA)','Congo red, apple-green birefringence under polarised light']]},
s:[['bio','In diabetes, non-enzymatic glycation of basement membrane proteins thickens and leaks; hyaline arteriolosclerosis of the efferent arteriole raises glomerular pressure, so diabetic kidneys first hyperfilter (raised GFR) before microalbuminuria and decline.']]},
{h:'Nephritic syndrome',p:[
'Inflammation of the glomerulus damages the barrier: **haematuria with red cell casts** (dysmorphic red cells), mild proteinuria (below 3.5 g/day), hypertension, oliguria and azotaemia.',
'**ANCA vasculitides** cause pauci-immune crescentic GN: **granulomatosis with polyangiitis** (PR3, c-ANCA; sinuses, lungs, kidneys), **microscopic polyangiitis** (MPO, p-ANCA; no granulomas) and **eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis** (MPO, asthma, eosinophilia). **Lupus nephritis** comes in classes: mesangial (II), focal or diffuse proliferative (III, IV, the most severe, with subendothelial "wire loops") and membranous (V, nephrotic). **Thin basement membrane disease** causes harmless familial microscopic haematuria.'],
table:{cols:['Disease','Clues','Biopsy'],rows:[
['Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis','children about 1–3 weeks after strep throat or 3–6 weeks after impetigo; cola urine, periorbital oedema, hypertension; low C3, raised ASO or anti-DNase B (after skin infection)','subepithelial "humps", granular IF; usually resolves in children'],
['IgA nephropathy (Berger)','haematuria 1–3 days after an upper respiratory or GI infection; commonest glomerulonephritis worldwide; IgA vasculitis','mesangial IgA'],
['Rapidly progressive GN','crescents (fibrin and macrophages in Bowman\'s space); kidney fails over weeks','type 1 anti-GBM (Goodpasture: linear IgG, lung haemorrhage); type 2 immune complex (lupus, PSGN, IgA); type 3 pauci-immune (ANCA vasculitis)'],
['Lupus nephritis (diffuse proliferative)','SLE','wire loops, subendothelial deposits, "full house" IF'],
['Membranoproliferative GN','hepatitis C and cryoglobulins (immune-complex type); C3 nephritic factor (C3 glomerulopathy, dense deposit disease)','"tram-track" split basement membrane'],
['Alport syndrome','type IV collagen (usually X-linked, COL4A5); haematuria, sensorineural deafness, lens problems','"basket-weave" split basement membrane']]},
s:[['path','Nephritic syndrome with low complement: post-streptococcal, lupus, membranoproliferative. With normal complement: IgA, anti-GBM, ANCA vasculitis.'],['micro','Only nephritogenic strains of group A streptococci cause PSGN. Penicillin treats the infection but, unlike rheumatic fever after pharyngitis, does not reliably prevent the nephritis.']]},
{h:'Where the deposits sit',p:[
'Immune deposits are named by where they lodge relative to the basement membrane. Antibodies against podocyte antigens (PLA2R) or small cationic antigens that cross the membrane build deposits on the podocyte side; large circulating complexes stick on the endothelial side or in the mesangium. Where they sit predicts the syndrome: subepithelial deposits injure podocytes (proteinuria), subendothelial deposits face the blood and recruit inflammatory cells (nephritis).'],
table:{cols:['Site','Diseases','Pattern'],rows:[
['Subepithelial','membranous nephropathy; post-streptococcal GN','spikes between deposits; large humps'],
['Subendothelial','lupus class IV; immune-complex MPGN','wire loops; tram tracks'],
['Mesangial','IgA nephropathy; lupus class II','mesangial IgA or IgG'],
['Within the basement membrane','dense deposit disease (C3 glomerulopathy)','ribbon-like dense deposits'],
['Linear along the membrane','anti-GBM disease','linear IgG'],
['None','minimal change; ANCA vasculitis (pauci-immune)','effaced foot processes; crescents']]}}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 5-year-old has periorbital oedema, 6 g/day proteinuria and a normal glomerulus on light microscopy. Expected course?',o:['Poor steroid response and progression to kidney failure','Remission with corticosteroids','Dialysis within weeks','Plasma exchange needed'],a:1,d:'path',c:'nephritic-nephrotic',x:'Heavy proteinuria with normal light microscopy in a young child is minimal change disease: cytokines efface the podocyte foot processes (seen only on EM) and the barrier loses its charge selectivity, so albumin leaks. Over 90% of children remit with steroids; a poor steroid response would point instead to FSGS.'},
{q:'Cola-coloured urine and hypertension four weeks after impetigo, with a low C3. Expected biopsy finding?',o:['Mesangial IgA deposits on immunofluorescence','Linear IgG along the basement membrane','Subepithelial humps','Crescents with no immune deposits at all'],a:2,d:'path',c:'nephritic-nephrotic',x:'Streptococcal antigens lodge in the glomerulus and immune complexes form subepithelial humps that activate complement (low C3), 3–6 weeks after impetigo or 1–3 weeks after pharyngitis. IgA nephropathy is the tempting distractor, but it flares within days of an infection, with normal C3.'},
{q:'A young man has haemoptysis and a rapidly rising creatinine; the biopsy shows linear IgG along the glomerular basement membrane. Target antigen?',o:['Phospholipase A2 receptor on podocytes','Myeloperoxidase in neutrophils','Proteinase 3 in neutrophils','Type IV collagen α3'],a:3,d:'path',c:'hypersensitivity',x:'Anti-GBM disease is a type II reaction: IgG against the α3 chain of type IV collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes gives linear immunofluorescence, crescents and lung haemorrhage; plasma exchange removes the antibody. MPO and PR3 are ANCA targets, which cause pauci-immune crescents with no linear IgG.'},
{q:'Which substance\'s clearance measures GFR most accurately?',o:['Inulin','Para-aminohippurate','Creatinine','Urea'],a:0,d:'phys',c:'gfr',x:'Inulin is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so the amount excreted equals the amount filtered and its clearance equals GFR. Creatinine is close but slightly overestimates GFR because a little is secreted; PAH is filtered and secreted almost completely, so it measures renal plasma flow instead.'},
{q:'An ACE inhibitor dilates which arteriole, and what happens to GFR?',o:['Afferent; GFR rises','Afferent; GFR falls','Efferent; GFR rises','Efferent; GFR falls'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'raas',x:'Angiotensin II keeps the efferent arteriole tight to hold glomerular pressure up; an ACE inhibitor relaxes it, so glomerular pressure, GFR and filtration fraction fall, and a small rise in creatinine is expected. That drop in intraglomerular pressure is why these drugs slow diabetic nephropathy, and why they can precipitate kidney failure in bilateral renal artery stenosis.'},
{q:'A drug constricts only the efferent arteriole, moderately. What happens to renal plasma flow, GFR and filtration fraction?',o:['RPF ↓, GFR ↓, FF unchanged','RPF ↓, GFR ↑, FF ↑','RPF ↑, GFR ↑, FF unchanged','RPF unchanged, GFR ↓, FF ↓'],a:1,d:'phys',c:'gfr',x:'Narrowing the outflow adds resistance in series, so less plasma flows, but pressure backs up inside the glomerulus, so GFR rises and FF (GFR/RPF) rises even more. Afferent constriction, as with NSAIDs, is the tempting answer but lowers RPF and GFR together and leaves FF unchanged.'},
{q:'A 55-year-old man with anti-PLA2R-positive nephrotic syndrome has sudden flank pain, haematuria and a jump in proteinuria. Most likely cause?',o:['Renal vein thrombosis','Renal cell carcinoma bleeding','Calcium oxalate ureteric stone','Acute ascending pyelonephritis'],a:0,d:'path',c:'nephritic-nephrotic',x:'Nephrotic syndrome loses antithrombin in the urine while the liver overmakes clotting factors, so patients clot easily, and membranous nephropathy carries the highest risk of renal vein thrombosis. A stone is the tempting answer for flank pain with haematuria, but would not explain the link to heavy proteinuria or the worsening leak.'}
]};

BOOK['c10-tubule']={depth:10,title:'The Long Loops',
epi:'The filtrate runs a long way through the dark: down a hairpin, up again, past the gates that decide what stays.',
c:['nephron-diuretics','raas','potassium','calcium-pth'],
S:[
{h:'Segment by segment',fig:'nephron',p:[
'**Proximal tubule** reabsorbs about two thirds of filtered Na⁺ and water, all glucose and amino acids (SGLT2 with Na⁺), most bicarbonate (carbonic anhydrase), phosphate (PTH reduces this) and urate; it secretes organic acids and drugs. Glucose appears in the urine when plasma glucose exceeds about 10–11 mmol/L (180–200 mg/dL). **Thin descending limb**: water out (concentrating). **Thick ascending limb**: **Na⁺/K⁺/2Cl⁻ cotransporter (NKCC2)**, impermeable to water (diluting), creates the lumen-positive potential that drives Mg²⁺ and Ca²⁺ reabsorption. **Distal convoluted tubule**: **Na⁺/Cl⁻ cotransporter**; PTH increases Ca²⁺ reabsorption. **Collecting duct**: principal cells reabsorb Na⁺ through **ENaC** and secrete K⁺ (aldosterone); water through **aquaporin-2** (ADH, V₂); intercalated cells secrete H⁺ (α) or HCO₃⁻ (β).',
'The proximal tubule has a **transport maximum** for glucose: once SGLT carriers are saturated, the excess spills into urine. Spill begins before every nephron is saturated (splay), which is why glycosuria starts below the full maximum. Angiotensin II speeds proximal Na⁺/H⁺ exchange, so volume loss also holds on to bicarbonate (contraction alkalosis). The proximal cells also make **ammonia** from glutamine; ammonium excreted in the urine carries acid out and regenerates bicarbonate, and this is the main way the kidney ramps up acid excretion in acidosis.'],
table:{cols:['Diuretic','Site','Effects and toxicity'],rows:[
['Acetazolamide','proximal tubule (carbonic anhydrase)','bicarbonate loss → hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis; glaucoma, altitude sickness, idiopathic intracranial hypertension'],
['Mannitol','osmotic, proximal and loop','raised intracranial pressure; pulmonary oedema risk'],
['Loop (furosemide, bumetanide)','thick ascending limb, NKCC2','↓K⁺, ↓Mg²⁺, ↓Ca²⁺ (used in hypercalcaemia), metabolic alkalosis, ototoxicity, gout; sulfa allergy (ethacrynic acid is not a sulfonamide)'],
['Thiazides (hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone)','DCT, NaCl cotransporter','↓K⁺, ↓Na⁺, metabolic alkalosis, ↑serum Ca²⁺ with ↓urinary Ca²⁺ (so used to prevent calcium stones and protective in osteoporosis), ↑glucose, ↑lipids, ↑urate; paradoxically helps nephrogenic DI'],
['K⁺-sparing (spironolactone, eplerenone; amiloride, triamterene)','collecting duct (aldosterone receptor; ENaC)','hyperkalaemia; spironolactone gynaecomastia; amiloride for lithium-induced DI']]},
key:'Loops lose calcium; thiazides keep it.',
s:[['path','Bartter syndrome mimics loop diuretics (NKCC2 or related); Gitelman syndrome mimics thiazides (NCC; low Mg²⁺ and low urinary Ca²⁺). Liddle syndrome: ENaC gain of function, hypertension with low K⁺ and low renin and aldosterone; amiloride. Fanconi syndrome: generalised proximal failure (glucose, amino acids, phosphate, bicarbonate lost); Wilson disease, myeloma, cystinosis, ifosfamide, tenofovir, outdated tetracyclines.'],['pharm','SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) block glucose and sodium reabsorption in the early proximal tubule: glycosuria, mild diuresis, weight loss, lower glomerular pressure via tubuloglomerular feedback, and fewer heart failure and kidney events. Risks: genital fungal infections, urinary infection, volume depletion and euglycaemic ketoacidosis.'],['bio','Carbonic anhydrase links the two sides of the proximal cell: H⁺ secreted into the lumen meets filtered HCO₃⁻, forms CO₂ and water (luminal enzyme), which enter the cell and are rebuilt into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ (cytoplasmic enzyme); the bicarbonate leaves into the blood.']]},
{h:'Concentrating the urine',p:[
'The loop of Henle sets up a medullary gradient (countercurrent multiplication), with urea recycling from the inner medullary collecting duct adding to it; the vasa recta (countercurrent exchange) carry blood through it without washing it away. ADH inserts aquaporin-2 channels and urea transporters so water can follow the gradient: urine can reach about 1,200 mOsm/kg. Without ADH, urine can be as dilute as about 50 mOsm/kg. Loop diuretics flatten the gradient, so the kidney can neither concentrate nor fully dilute.',
'**Diabetes insipidus** (now also called arginine vasopressin deficiency for the central form and arginine vasopressin resistance for the nephrogenic form) gives large volumes of dilute urine with a high-normal plasma sodium as long as thirst works. **Free water clearance** is positive when the urine is more dilute than plasma and negative when it is concentrated, as in SIADH.'],
s:[['phys','Water deprivation test: in central DI, urine stays dilute until desmopressin is given (it then concentrates). In nephrogenic DI (lithium, demeclocycline, hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia, V₂ or aquaporin mutations), desmopressin does nothing. Primary polydipsia: urine concentrates with deprivation.']]},
{h:'Hormones that tune the tubule',p:[
'Four hormones adjust what the nephron keeps. Read them by the problem each one solves: angiotensin II and aldosterone defend volume, ADH defends osmolality, PTH defends calcium, and the natriuretic peptides vent excess volume.'],
table:{cols:['Hormone','Where it acts','Effect'],rows:[
['Angiotensin II','efferent arteriole; proximal tubule','holds GFR up in low flow; more Na⁺/H⁺ exchange (Na⁺ and HCO₃⁻ kept)'],
['Aldosterone','principal and α-intercalated cells','more ENaC and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase: Na⁺ kept, K⁺ and H⁺ secreted'],
['ADH (vasopressin)','V₂ receptors in the collecting duct; V₁ on vessels','aquaporin-2 and urea transporters inserted: water kept; vasoconstriction at high levels'],
['PTH','proximal tubule; distal convoluted tubule','less phosphate reabsorbed; more Ca²⁺ reabsorbed; more 1α-hydroxylase (calcitriol)'],
['ANP and BNP','afferent and efferent arterioles; collecting duct','GFR rises and Na⁺ reabsorption falls (natriuresis); renin and aldosterone suppressed']]}}
],
Q:[
{q:'Which diuretic lowers urinary calcium and helps prevent calcium stones?',o:['Furosemide','Hydrochlorothiazide','Acetazolamide','Mannitol'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'nephron-diuretics',x:'Blocking NaCl entry in the distal convoluted tubule lowers the cell\'s Na⁺, which speeds basolateral Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchange and pulls more Ca²⁺ out of the lumen, while mild volume depletion raises proximal Ca²⁺ reabsorption, so urinary calcium falls. Furosemide does the opposite: it abolishes the lumen-positive voltage that drives Ca²⁺ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb.'},
{q:'A young man has hypertension, low K⁺, low renin and low aldosterone; his pressure falls with amiloride but not spironolactone. Diagnosis?',o:['Liddle syndrome','Conn syndrome','Bartter syndrome','Gitelman syndrome'],a:0,d:'path',c:'potassium',x:'A gain-of-function mutation keeps ENaC in the principal-cell membrane, so Na⁺ is reabsorbed without aldosterone: volume expansion suppresses renin and aldosterone, and K⁺ and H⁺ are lost. Amiloride blocks the channel, while spironolactone fails because no aldosterone is driving it; Conn syndrome looks similar but has high aldosterone.'},
{q:'Which segment is impermeable to water and dilutes the filtrate?',o:['Proximal tubule','Thin descending limb','Thick ascending limb','Collecting duct with ADH present'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'nephron-diuretics',x:'NKCC2 pumps Na⁺, K⁺ and 2Cl⁻ out of the lumen while the membrane stays watertight, so fluid leaving this segment is hypotonic (about 100 mOsm/kg) whatever the hydration state, and the salt builds the medullary gradient. The thin descending limb is the reverse: permeable to water but not to salt.'},
{q:'A patient has glucose, amino acids and phosphate in the urine with a normal blood glucose and a normal-gap acidosis. Which segment is failing?',o:['Proximal tubule','Loop of Henle','Distal convoluted tubule','Collecting duct'],a:0,d:'phys',c:'rta',x:'Glucose, amino acids, phosphate and bicarbonate are all reclaimed in the proximal tubule, so losing all of them together is Fanconi syndrome (with type 2 RTA and hypophosphataemic rickets or osteomalacia); causes include cystinosis, Wilson disease, myeloma light chains, ifosfamide and tenofovir. A distal defect would not waste glucose or amino acids.'},
{q:'A patient on lithium has polyuria that does not respond to desmopressin. Best drug if lithium must continue?',o:['Furosemide','More desmopressin','Amiloride','Spironolactone'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'water-balance',x:'Lithium enters principal cells through ENaC and disrupts ADH signalling, causing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus; amiloride blocks that entry. Thiazides also help by causing mild volume depletion and more proximal water reabsorption, whereas extra desmopressin fails because the receptor pathway, not the hormone, has failed.'},
{q:'A man with type 2 diabetes starts a drug that causes glycosuria, weight loss and recurrent genital thrush, and later ketoacidosis with a glucose of 9 mmol/L. Mechanism?',o:['Stimulates insulin release from β cells','Blocks NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb','Blocks ENaC in the collecting duct','Blocks SGLT2 in the proximal tubule'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'diabetes',x:'SGLT2 reclaims most filtered glucose in the early proximal tubule; blocking it causes glycosuria (feeding genital fungi), lowers insulin needs and shifts metabolism towards ketones, so ketoacidosis can arise with near-normal glucose (euglycaemic DKA). Sulfonylureas stimulate insulin release and cause hypoglycaemia, not glycosuria.'},
{q:'After 8 hours without water, urine osmolality stays at 150 mOsm/kg; after desmopressin it rises to 600 mOsm/kg. Diagnosis?',o:['Primary polydipsia','Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus','SIADH','Central diabetes insipidus'],a:3,d:'phys',c:'water-balance',x:'The kidney cannot concentrate without ADH but responds normally once desmopressin is given, so the defect lies in the hypothalamus or posterior pituitary (after head injury, surgery, tumours, or idiopathic): central DI. In nephrogenic DI desmopressin changes little, and in primary polydipsia the urine concentrates during deprivation itself.'}
]};

BOOK['c10-salts']={depth:10,title:'The Salt Ledgers',
epi:'Clerks in the walls count every grain of salt. The Salt Witch has been changing their numbers.',
c:['water-balance','potassium','raas','calcium-pth','adrenal'],
S:[
{h:'Sodium is water',p:[
'Plasma sodium reflects **water** balance; total body sodium sets **volume**. First check **serum osmolality**. It is high in hyperglycaemia or after mannitol (water is pulled out of cells; sodium falls about 1.6–2.4 mmol/L for each 5.5 mmol/L (100 mg/dL) rise in glucose) and normal in pseudohyponatraemia from very high lipids or protein. Only low-osmolality (hypotonic) hyponatraemia is then sorted by volume status.',
'**Hypovolaemic**: diuretics (thiazides), vomiting, diarrhoea, adrenal insufficiency (urine Na⁺ high if renal loss). **Euvolaemic**: **SIADH** (small cell carcinoma, CNS disease, lung disease, drugs such as SSRIs, carbamazepine, cyclophosphamide; concentrated urine with urine Na⁺ above 40), hypothyroidism, glucocorticoid deficiency, primary polydipsia and beer potomania (very dilute urine). **Hypervolaemic**: heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome (low effective circulating volume makes ADH and aldosterone rise even though the body is overfilled).',
'Correct chronic hyponatraemia slowly (no more than about 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours) to avoid **osmotic demyelination** (central pontine myelinolysis). **Hypernatraemia** means too little water: lost water without access to drink (infants, older people with dementia, unconscious patients), diabetes insipidus, osmotic diuresis, or rarely salt loading. Correct chronic hypernatraemia slowly too, or cerebral oedema follows.'],
table:{cols:['Hypotonic hyponatraemia','Urine Na⁺','Typical causes'],rows:[
['Hypovolaemic, extrarenal loss','below 20','vomiting, diarrhoea, burns, sweating'],
['Hypovolaemic, renal loss','above 20','diuretics, Addison disease, salt-wasting nephropathy'],
['Euvolaemic','above 40 (SIADH); variable','SIADH, hypothyroidism, glucocorticoid deficiency; polydipsia gives urine below 100 mOsm/kg'],
['Hypervolaemic','below 20 (above 20 in kidney failure)','heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, advanced kidney failure']]},
s:[['pharm','SIADH: fluid restriction first; then salt tablets, tolvaptan or conivaptan (V₂ antagonists; tolvaptan can injure the liver and overcorrect), demeclocycline. Severe symptomatic hyponatraemia (seizures, coma): small boluses of 3% saline to raise sodium by a few mmol/L.'],['path','Osmotic demyelination: brain cells that shed osmolytes to adapt to chronic hyponatraemia shrink when sodium rises fast; oligodendrocytes die and myelin is lost symmetrically in the central pons (and sometimes elsewhere), with axons spared. Two to six days later: dysarthria, dysphagia, quadriparesis, even a locked-in state. Alcohol use, malnutrition, hypokalaemia and liver disease raise the risk.']]},
{h:'Potassium shifts',p:[
'**Into cells** (plasma K⁺ falls): insulin, β₂ agonists, alkalosis, hypo-osmolality. **Out of cells** (plasma K⁺ rises): insulin deficiency, β-blockers, acidosis, hyperosmolality (hyperglycaemia), digoxin (pump blocked), cell lysis (rhabdomyolysis, tumour lysis, haemolysis), succinylcholine. Renal causes of high K⁺: kidney failure, low aldosterone (ACE inhibitors, ARBs, spironolactone, type 4 RTA, Addison), trimethoprim, amiloride. A haemolysed sample gives a falsely high result.',
'**Low magnesium** causes renal potassium wasting; replace magnesium first or potassium will not correct. Potassium sets the resting membrane potential: low K⁺ hyperpolarises and slows repolarisation (U waves, arrhythmias, weakness, ileus, nephrogenic DI), while high K⁺ depolarises cells and first speeds, then blocks conduction (peaked T waves, wide QRS, sine waves, asystole).'],
table:{cols:['Electrolyte','Low','High'],rows:[
['Na⁺','confusion, seizures, coma','irritability, stupor, coma'],
['K⁺','U waves, flat T, weakness, ileus, arrhythmia','peaked T, wide QRS, arrhythmia, weakness'],
['Ca²⁺','tetany, Chvostek and Trousseau signs, long QT, seizures','kidney stones, bone pain, abdominal pain and constipation, polyuria, low mood and confusion; short QT'],
['Mg²⁺','tetany, torsades, low K⁺ and Ca²⁺','weak reflexes, hypotension, respiratory depression (magnesium sulfate in pre-eclampsia)'],
['PO₄³⁻','refeeding syndrome, weakness, haemolysis, rhabdomyolysis','metastatic calcification, CKD, low Ca²⁺']]},
s:[['pharm','Hyperkalaemia with ECG changes: IV calcium gluconate first (stabilises the myocardium within minutes but does not lower K⁺); then shift K⁺ into cells with insulin plus glucose and nebulised salbutamol; then remove it with binders (sodium zirconium cyclosilicate, patiromer), loop diuretics or dialysis. Bicarbonate helps mainly when acidosis is present.']]},
{h:'Renin, angiotensin, aldosterone',p:[
'Low renal perfusion, low NaCl at the **macula densa**, and β₁ stimulation release **renin** from juxtaglomerular cells. Renin cuts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I; **ACE** (lung endothelium) makes angiotensin II, which constricts arterioles (efferent most), stimulates aldosterone and ADH, thirst, proximal Na⁺ reabsorption, and sympathetic activity. ACE also breaks down bradykinin.',
'**Aldosterone** acts on principal cells: more ENaC and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, so Na⁺ reabsorbed and K⁺ and H⁺ secreted. **ANP and BNP** (released from stretched atria and ventricles) oppose the system: they raise cGMP, dilate the afferent arteriole, cause natriuresis and suppress renin. ADH also responds to volume (strongly when volume falls a lot). Escape from aldosterone means that primary hyperaldosteronism raises blood pressure but rarely causes oedema: pressure natriuresis and ANP limit the sodium gain.'],
s:[['phys','ADH answers two masters. Hypothalamic osmoreceptors release it when plasma osmolality rises by as little as 1–2%; baroreceptors release it only after volume falls by about 10% or more, but then override osmolality, which is why hypovolaemia causes hyponatraemia.'],['pharm','ACE inhibitors: cough and angioedema (bradykinin), hyperkalaemia, first-dose hypotension, acute kidney injury in bilateral renal artery stenosis, teratogenic. ARBs share most of this without the cough. Aliskiren inhibits renin directly. Sacubitril blocks neprilysin, the enzyme that breaks down natriuretic peptides; it is paired with valsartan, never with an ACE inhibitor (angioedema), and needs a 36-hour gap after one.'],['bio','Angiotensinogen is made in the liver; renin is an aspartyl protease and its cleavage of angiotensinogen is the rate-limiting step of the cascade. ACE (kininase II) is a zinc-dependent dipeptidyl carboxypeptidase that removes two amino acids from angiotensin I and inactivates bradykinin.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A smoker with a lung mass is euvolaemic with Na⁺ 118 mmol/L, urine osmolality 600 mOsm/kg and urine Na⁺ 60 mmol/L. Diagnosis?',o:['Primary polydipsia','Heart failure with low effective volume','Thiazide-induced hyponatraemia','SIADH'],a:3,d:'path',c:'water-balance',x:'Euvolaemia with inappropriately concentrated urine while the plasma is dilute, plus urine Na⁺ above 40, means ADH is being secreted without a physiological trigger, here ectopically by the tumour. Primary polydipsia gives maximally dilute urine (below 100 mOsm/kg), and a thiazide would usually leave the patient volume-depleted with the drug in the history.'},
{q:'A malnourished man who drinks heavily has Na⁺ 108 mmol/L; fluids raise it to 124 in 24 hours. Three days later he is dysarthric and quadriparetic. Lesion?',o:['Cerebral oedema from water entering cells','Demyelination in the central pons','Wernicke lesions in the mammillary bodies','Hypocalcaemic injury'],a:1,d:'path',c:'water-balance',x:'A rise of 16 mmol/L in a day far exceeds the 8–10 limit: brain cells that had shed osmolytes to cope with chronic hyponatraemia shrink, and oligodendrocytes in the pons die (osmotic demyelination), with a delay of 2–6 days. Cerebral oedema is the danger of acute hyponatraemia itself or of correcting hypernatraemia too fast.'},
{q:'Hypokalaemia keeps returning despite oral potassium. Which level should be checked?',o:['Ionised calcium','Phosphate','Magnesium','Chloride and bicarbonate'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'potassium',x:'Intracellular Mg²⁺ normally restrains ROMK potassium channels in the collecting duct; when magnesium is low, potassium leaks into the urine continuously, so K⁺ replacement alone fails until Mg²⁺ is restored. Low Mg²⁺ also lowers PTH release and action, which is why hypocalcaemia, the tempting first option, often travels with it.'},
{q:'Where is most angiotensin I converted to angiotensin II?',o:['Pulmonary capillary endothelium','Kidney tubule','Liver','Adrenal cortex'],a:0,d:'phys',c:'raas',x:'ACE sits on endothelium everywhere, but the lungs\' huge capillary bed receives the whole cardiac output, so most conversion happens there. The liver makes angiotensinogen and the kidney makes renin, which makes them the tempting distractors; the adrenal cortex responds to angiotensin II rather than making it.'},
{q:'Which antibiotic can cause hyperkalaemia by blocking ENaC in the same way as amiloride?',o:['Ciprofloxacin','Trimethoprim','Nitrofurantoin','Amoxicillin'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'potassium',x:'Trimethoprim is structurally like amiloride and blocks ENaC in principal cells, so less Na⁺ enters and the lumen-negative voltage that drives K⁺ secretion collapses; serum K⁺ rises, especially at high doses or with ACE inhibitors or spironolactone. It also blocks creatinine secretion, so creatinine can rise without a real fall in GFR.'},
{q:'A man with uncontrolled diabetes has glucose 44 mmol/L (about 800 mg/dL), Na⁺ 126 mmol/L and serum osmolality 305 mOsm/kg. Best explanation of the low sodium?',o:['SIADH from the stress of illness','Pseudohyponatraemia from lipaemia','Water pulled out of cells','Excess free water intake from thirst'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'water-balance',x:'Glucose stays outside cells and pulls water into the plasma, diluting sodium while osmolality stays high (hypertonic hyponatraemia); correcting for the glucose adds roughly 11–17 mmol/L, so the true sodium is normal. SIADH and excess water give low osmolality, and pseudohyponatraemia from lipids or protein gives normal osmolality.'},
{q:'K⁺ is 7.1 mmol/L with peaked T waves and a widening QRS. Which drug comes first?',o:['Insulin with glucose','Sodium zirconium cyclosilicate','Furosemide','Intravenous calcium gluconate'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'potassium',x:'Calcium does not lower potassium, but within minutes it restores the gap between resting and threshold potential and protects the heart, so it comes first when the ECG has changed. Insulin with glucose is the tempting answer and is given next to shift K⁺ into cells, but it takes 15–30 minutes and does not stabilise the myocardium; binders and diuretics are slower still.'}
]};

BOOK['c10-acid']={depth:10,title:'The Acid Wells',
epi:'The wells here bubble with acid. The kidneys and lungs fight to keep the pH between narrow walls.',
c:['acid-base','rta','diabetes','ethanol','toxicology'],
S:[
{h:'Reading a blood gas',fig:'abg',p:[
'Henderson-Hasselbalch: **pH = 6.1 + log [HCO₃⁻ / (0.03 × PaCO₂)]**. Step 1: acidaemia (pH < 7.35) or alkalaemia (> 7.45). Step 2: is the primary change in PaCO₂ (respiratory) or HCO₃⁻ (metabolic)? Step 3: check compensation. Step 4: in metabolic acidosis, calculate the **anion gap** = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻), normal about 8–12 (it falls by about 2.5 for each 10 g/L drop in albumin).',
'Step 5: in a high-gap acidosis, compare the rise in the gap with the fall in bicarbonate (the **delta ratio**). Roughly equal means a pure high-gap acidosis; a bicarbonate higher than expected means a metabolic alkalosis is hiding underneath (vomiting in DKA), and lower than expected means a normal-gap acidosis is added. Step 6: if a toxic alcohol is suspected, the **osmolar gap** (measured minus calculated osmolality, normal below about 10) is high while the parent alcohol is still circulating. Compensation never overshoots and rarely brings pH fully back to normal; a normal pH with abnormal numbers means two disorders.'],
table:{cols:['Disorder','Expected compensation'],rows:[
['Metabolic acidosis','Winter\'s formula: PaCO₂ = 1.5 × HCO₃⁻ + 8 ± 2 (mmHg)'],
['Metabolic alkalosis','PaCO₂ rises about 0.7 mmHg per 1 mmol/L rise in HCO₃⁻'],
['Acute respiratory acidosis','HCO₃⁻ rises 1 per 10 mmHg rise in PaCO₂'],
['Chronic respiratory acidosis','HCO₃⁻ rises 3.5 per 10 mmHg'],
['Acute respiratory alkalosis','HCO₃⁻ falls 2 per 10 mmHg'],
['Chronic respiratory alkalosis','HCO₃⁻ falls 4–5 per 10 mmHg']]},
key:'If the PaCO₂ is not where Winter\'s formula predicts, a second (respiratory) disorder is present.'},
{h:'Causes',p:[
'**High anion gap metabolic acidosis** has four wells of unmeasured acid: **ketones** (diabetic, alcoholic, starvation ketoacidosis), **lactate** (L-lactate from shock and hypoxia; D-lactate from bacteria in a short bowel), **kidney failure** (sulfate, phosphate, urate retained), and **toxins** (methanol, ethylene glycol, propylene glycol, salicylates, iron, isoniazid, and oxoproline (pyroglutamic acid) from chronic paracetamol use, especially in malnourished patients). Toluene usually gives a normal-gap acidosis because its hippurate is excreted quickly.',
'**Normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic)**: diarrhoea, renal tubular acidosis, acetazolamide, large saline infusions, ureteral diversion into bowel, Addison disease. The **urine anion gap** (Na⁺ + K⁺ − Cl⁻) separates the two commonest: negative when the kidney is excreting plenty of ammonium (diarrhoea), positive when it cannot (distal RTA).',
'**Metabolic alkalosis**: vomiting and nasogastric suction (chloride-responsive, low urine Cl⁻), diuretics (urine Cl⁻ high while the drug acts, low once it wears off), hyperaldosteronism and Cushing (chloride-resistant, high urine Cl⁻), antacid overuse (milk-alkali), Bartter and Gitelman. **Respiratory acidosis**: hypoventilation (opioids, COPD, neuromuscular disease, obesity). **Respiratory alkalosis**: hyperventilation (anxiety, PE, early salicylate toxicity, altitude, pregnancy, sepsis, cirrhosis).'],
table:{cols:['Renal tubular acidosis','Defect','Clues'],rows:[
['Type 1 (distal)','α-intercalated cells cannot secrete H⁺','urine pH > 5.5 even in acidaemia, low K⁺, calcium phosphate stones and nephrocalcinosis; Sjögren, amphotericin, lupus; treat with citrate or bicarbonate'],
['Type 2 (proximal)','bicarbonate not reabsorbed','urine pH < 5.5 once plasma HCO₃⁻ falls; low K⁺; Fanconi syndrome, acetazolamide, myeloma; rickets'],
['Type 4 (hyperkalaemic)','low aldosterone or resistance','high K⁺, urine pH < 5.5; diabetes, ACE inhibitors, spironolactone, trimethoprim']]},
s:[['pharm','Toxic alcohols: fomepizole (or ethanol) blocks alcohol dehydrogenase so the toxic acids are never made; haemodialysis removes the alcohol and its acids. Salicylate poisoning: alkalinise the urine with bicarbonate (ion trapping speeds excretion) and dialyse if severe.']]},
{h:'Ketoacidosis, lactate and poisons',p:[
'**DKA**: insulin deficiency lets lipolysis and ketogenesis run (β-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate). Hyperglycaemia, high anion gap acidosis, Kussmaul breathing, fruity breath, abdominal pain, dehydration; total body K⁺ is low even when serum K⁺ is high (acidosis and insulin deficiency shift it out). Treat with fluids, insulin, and potassium replacement as it falls; bicarbonate only for extreme acidosis. **Lactic acidosis**: shock, sepsis, metformin with kidney failure, cyanide, thiamine deficiency, seizures.',
'**Salicylates** first stimulate the respiratory centre (respiratory alkalosis), then uncouple oxidative phosphorylation (lactic and keto acids): a mixed picture with tinnitus, vomiting, fever and confusion. **Methanol** becomes formic acid, which poisons the retina and optic nerve ("snowstorm" vision, blindness) and the basal ganglia. **Ethylene glycol** (antifreeze) becomes glycolic and oxalic acid: calcium oxalate crystals, kidney failure and low calcium.'],
table:{cols:['Poison','Clues','Treatment'],rows:[
['Methanol','visual blurring, optic disc swelling; high anion and osmolar gaps','fomepizole, dialysis; folate helps clear formate'],
['Ethylene glycol','drunk without the smell, flank pain, envelope-shaped calcium oxalate crystals, AKI','fomepizole, dialysis'],
['Salicylate','tinnitus, vomiting, hyperventilation; mixed respiratory alkalosis and high-gap acidosis','bicarbonate (alkalinise urine), dialysis'],
['Isopropyl alcohol','drunk, ketones without acidosis; high osmolar gap only','supportive (no toxic acid to block)']]},
s:[['phys','Urine ketone dipsticks detect acetoacetate, not β-hydroxybutyrate; early DKA can test falsely low.'],['path','Alcoholic ketoacidosis follows a binge with vomiting and no food: glucose is normal or low, and the high NADH of ethanol metabolism pushes acetoacetate to β-hydroxybutyrate, so dipsticks underread the ketones. Give thiamine before glucose, then dextrose and fluids; insulin is rarely needed.'],['bio','In the liver mitochondria, fatty acids are cut by β-oxidation into acetyl-CoA. When oxaloacetate is drained for gluconeogenesis, acetyl-CoA cannot enter the citric acid cycle; it is condensed into HMG-CoA (by HMG-CoA synthase, the ketogenic step) and split into acetoacetate, which is reduced to β-hydroxybutyrate (more so when NADH is high, as in alcohol use) or loses CO₂ to become acetone. The liver lacks the thiophorase needed to use ketones, so it exports them to brain, heart and muscle.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'pH 7.29, HCO₃⁻ 12 mmol/L, PaCO₂ 26 mmHg. What PaCO₂ does Winter\'s formula predict, and what is the conclusion?',o:['40: a respiratory acidosis too','26 ± 2: appropriate compensation','18: a respiratory alkalosis too','35: a metabolic alkalosis too'],a:1,d:'phys',c:'acid-base',x:'Winter\'s formula predicts PaCO₂ = 1.5 × 12 + 8 = 26 ± 2 mmHg, and the measured 26 is on target, so this is a simple metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation. A PaCO₂ above 28 would reveal a coexisting respiratory acidosis, and one below 24 a coexisting respiratory alkalosis.'},
{q:'After a week of diarrhoea: Na⁺ 140, Cl⁻ 115, HCO₃⁻ 15 mmol/L. Anion gap and type of disorder?',o:['10: normal-gap (hyperchloraemic) acidosis','25: high-gap acidosis','10: metabolic alkalosis','5: low gap from hypoalbuminaemia'],a:0,d:'phys',c:'acid-base',x:'140 − (115 + 15) = 10, a normal gap: stool loses HCO₃⁻ and the kidney holds on to Cl⁻ to keep electroneutrality, so chloride rises as bicarbonate falls. A high gap would need an unmeasured acid such as lactate or ketones; here the urine anion gap is negative because the kidney excretes plenty of ammonium, unlike in distal RTA.'},
{q:'After 3 days of vomiting, which disorder is present and what is the urine chloride?',o:['Metabolic acidosis; urine Cl⁻ high','Metabolic alkalosis; urine Cl⁻ high','Respiratory alkalosis; urine Cl⁻ high','Metabolic alkalosis; urine Cl⁻ low'],a:3,d:'phys',c:'acid-base',x:'Vomiting removes HCl, and volume and chloride depletion keep the kidney reabsorbing Na⁺ with HCO₃⁻ while aldosterone drives H⁺ and K⁺ loss, so the alkalosis persists; urine Cl⁻ is low (below about 20) and saline corrects it. A high urine Cl⁻ points instead to a diuretic still acting, mineralocorticoid excess, or Bartter or Gitelman syndrome.'},
{q:'A woman with Sjögren syndrome has low K⁺, a normal-gap acidosis, urine pH 6.5 and calcium phosphate stones. RTA type?',o:['Type 2 (proximal)','Type 4 (hyperkalaemic)','Type 1 (distal)','No RTA: diarrhoea'],a:2,d:'path',c:'rta',x:'α-Intercalated cells cannot secrete H⁺, so urine pH stays above 5.5 even in acidaemia; bone buffering releases calcium phosphate, low urinary citrate lets it precipitate into stones, and K⁺ is wasted. Proximal (type 2) RTA can still acidify the urine once plasma bicarbonate falls low enough, and rarely causes stones.'},
{q:'In DKA with a serum K⁺ of 5.6 mmol/L, what is total body potassium usually?',o:['High, matching the serum level','Low, despite the high serum level','Normal','Cannot be estimated without urine K⁺'],a:1,d:'phys',c:'diabetes',x:'Insulin lack and hyperosmolality push K⁺ out of cells, so serum K⁺ looks high, while osmotic diuresis has been wasting it in the urine for days: total body potassium is low. Once insulin drives K⁺ back into cells, the serum level falls fast, so potassium is added to the fluids once it drops below about 5–5.5 mmol/L, and insulin is held back if it is below about 3.3.'},
{q:'A confused man has pH 7.10, an anion gap of 28, an osmolar gap of 30 and envelope-shaped crystals in his urine. Toxin and antidote?',o:['Ethylene glycol; fomepizole','Methanol; folate alone','Salicylate; sodium bicarbonate','Paracetamol; N-acetylcysteine'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'toxicology',x:'Alcohol dehydrogenase turns ethylene glycol into glycolic and oxalic acid: a high anion gap, a high osmolar gap from the unmetabolised alcohol, and calcium oxalate crystals that injure the tubules. Fomepizole blocks the enzyme and dialysis removes glycol and acids; methanol also raises both gaps but attacks the optic nerve and leaves no crystals.'},
{q:'A woman who swallowed many aspirin tablets has tinnitus, pH 7.42, PaCO₂ 24 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 15 mmol/L and an anion gap of 22. Interpretation?',o:['Simple metabolic acidosis, fully compensated','Simple respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation','Laboratory error: the pH cannot be normal','Two disorders: acidosis plus alkalosis'],a:3,d:'phys',c:'acid-base',x:'Salicylate stimulates the respiratory centre directly and uncouples oxidative phosphorylation, adding lactic and keto acids; Winter\'s formula predicts a PaCO₂ of about 30, so 24 is lower than compensation alone explains. Compensation never fully normalises the pH, so a normal pH with abnormal numbers means two disorders, not one fully compensated one.'}
]};

BOOK['c10-failure']={depth:10,title:'The Silent Filters',
epi:'Whole tunnels have gone quiet. Nothing flows. The salt crusts thicken in the dark.',
c:['aki','ckd','stones','polycystic-kidney','nephritic-nephrotic','gout-purines','calcium-pth','anemia'],
S:[
{h:'Acute kidney injury',p:[
'**Prerenal** (low perfusion: dehydration, heart failure, NSAIDs with ACE inhibitors, hepatorenal syndrome): BUN/creatinine ratio above 20, urine Na⁺ below 20, fractional excretion of Na⁺ below 1% (fractional excretion of urea below 35% if the patient is on diuretics), concentrated urine, bland sediment. **Intrinsic**: **acute tubular necrosis** (ischaemia, aminoglycosides, contrast, myoglobin, cisplatin; **muddy brown granular casts**, FENa above 2%), acute interstitial nephritis (drugs such as penicillins, NSAIDs, PPIs, diuretics: fever, rash, blood eosinophilia, sterile pyuria and white cell casts; urine eosinophils are a weak test), glomerulonephritis (red cell casts). **Postrenal** (obstruction: prostate, stones, tumours): bilateral hydronephrosis.',
'The **straight proximal tubule** and the **medullary thick ascending limb** die first in ischaemia, because they work hardest in the least oxygenated zone. ATN runs in three phases: an initiating insult, a **maintenance** phase of low urine output with rising creatinine, hyperkalaemia and acidosis, and a **recovery** phase of polyuria in which regenerating tubules cannot yet concentrate, so potassium and water are lost. **Renal papillary necrosis** (sloughed papillae, colicky pain and haematuria) follows diabetes, analgesic overuse, sickle cell disease and severe pyelonephritis.'],
table:{cols:['Cast','Meaning'],rows:[['Red cell','glomerulonephritis'],['White cell','pyelonephritis, interstitial nephritis'],['Muddy brown granular','acute tubular necrosis'],['Waxy (broad)','chronic kidney disease'],['Fatty (oval fat bodies)','nephrotic syndrome'],['Hyaline','normal or dehydration']]},
s:[['path','Rhabdomyolysis (crush, prolonged immobility, statins, seizures, extreme exertion): very high CK, dipstick positive for blood but no red cells (myoglobin), hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia, early hypocalcaemia, AKI; aggressive fluids.'],['pharm','Common nephrotoxins: aminoglycosides (proximal tubule, often non-oliguric, days into therapy), amphotericin B (distal RTA, low K⁺ and Mg²⁺), cisplatin (prevent with hydration and amifostine), iodinated contrast, vancomycin, NSAIDs, ciclosporin and tacrolimus (afferent constriction), tenofovir (Fanconi), lithium (nephrogenic DI). Adjust renally cleared drugs to the GFR.']]},
{h:'Chronic kidney disease',p:[
'CKD is kidney damage or an eGFR below 60 mL/min/1.73 m² for more than 3 months, staged by GFR (G1–G5) and albuminuria (A1–A3). Causes: **diabetes** (commonest), hypertension, glomerulonephritis, polycystic kidney disease. Consequences: **anaemia** (low erythropoietin), **renal osteodystrophy** (phosphate retained → low Ca²⁺, low calcitriol → secondary hyperparathyroidism → bone resorption, osteitis fibrosa cystica), metabolic acidosis, hyperkalaemia, hypertension, uraemia (pericarditis, encephalopathy, asterixis, platelet dysfunction, itching), accelerated cardiovascular disease. Treat the cause; ACE inhibitors or ARBs and SGLT2 inhibitors slow progression; phosphate binders, calcitriol and cinacalcet; erythropoietin; dialysis or transplant.',
'Dialysis is needed urgently when the internal environment can no longer be held: **acidosis** that does not respond to treatment, dangerous **hyperkalaemia**, **dialysable poisons** (methanol, ethylene glycol, lithium, salicylates), **fluid overload** resistant to diuretics, and **uraemic complications** such as pericarditis, encephalopathy or bleeding. Tertiary hyperparathyroidism follows years of stimulation, when the glands become autonomous and calcium rises.'],
s:[['phys','Creatinine rises only after about half of the nephrons are lost, because the survivors hyperfilter; eGFR equations use creatinine (or cystatin C), age and sex, and are unreliable in AKI before a steady state is reached.'],['bio','The kidney\'s 1α-hydroxylase in the proximal tubule turns 25-hydroxyvitamin D into calcitriol; FGF23 from bone switches it off and dumps phosphate. In CKD FGF23 climbs early, so calcitriol falls before phosphate visibly rises.']]},
{h:'Stones',p:[
'Most stones are **calcium oxalate** (hypercalciuria; Crohn disease and small bowel resection raise oxalate absorption because free fat binds calcium in the gut; ethylene glycol, vitamin C excess). **Calcium phosphate** forms in alkaline urine (distal RTA, hyperparathyroidism). **Struvite** follows urease-producing organisms (Proteus, Klebsiella) that split urea into ammonia: staghorn calculi in alkaline urine. **Uric acid** stones are radiolucent and form in acidic urine (gout, tumour lysis, chronic diarrhoea). **Cystine** stones come from an autosomal recessive failure to reabsorb cystine, ornithine, lysine and arginine.',
'Stones cause colicky flank pain radiating to the groin, haematuria, and obstruction with infection (an emergency that needs drainage). Non-contrast CT finds nearly all of them. Prevention: plenty of fluid; thiazides for hypercalciuria; citrate (it binds calcium and alkalinises); low sodium and normal calcium diet (low-calcium diets raise oxalate absorption).'],
table:{cols:['Stone','X-ray','Urine','Clues'],rows:[
['Calcium oxalate','opaque','any pH','envelope or dumbbell crystals; commonest; hypercalciuria, Crohn, ethylene glycol'],
['Calcium phosphate','opaque','alkaline','distal RTA, primary hyperparathyroidism'],
['Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate)','opaque','alkaline','coffin-lid crystals; urease organisms; staghorn; remove stone and treat infection'],
['Uric acid','lucent on X-ray, seen on CT','acidic','rhomboid crystals; gout, tumour lysis; alkalinise, allopurinol'],
['Cystine','faintly opaque','acidic','hexagonal crystals; positive nitroprusside test; alkalinise, chelate (tiopronin, penicillamine)']]},
s:[['pharm','Allopurinol for recurrent uric acid or calcium oxalate stones with hyperuricosuria. Potassium citrate raises urine pH and citrate, which prevents uric acid, cystine and calcium stones.']]},
{h:'Cysts, tumours and infection',p:[
'**Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease** (PKD1 on chromosome 16, PKD2): enlarged kidneys with cysts in adults, flank pain, haematuria, hypertension, liver cysts, **berry aneurysms**, mitral prolapse; tolvaptan slows it. **Autosomal recessive** PKD (PKHD1): infants with huge kidneys, Potter sequence, congenital hepatic fibrosis. Simple cysts are common and harmless; multiple acquired cysts develop in long-term dialysis.',
'**Renal cell carcinoma** (proximal tubule; clear cell with VHL loss on chromosome 3p, so HIF drives VEGF): smoking, obesity; haematuria, flank pain, mass; paraneoplastic EPO (polycythaemia), PTHrP, renin; invades the renal vein (left varicocele, as the left gonadal vein drains into it) and spreads to lung and bone; treated by surgery, tyrosine kinase inhibitors and checkpoint inhibitors. **Angiomyolipoma** (vessels, smooth muscle and fat) is linked with tuberous sclerosis; **oncocytoma** is benign with mitochondria-packed cells and a central scar. **Wilms tumour**: children 2–4 years, large unilateral flank mass; WT1; WAGR and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndromes. **Urothelial carcinoma** of the bladder: painless haematuria; smoking, aniline dyes, cyclophosphamide, phenacetin. **Pyelonephritis**: fever, flank pain, white cell casts; usually ascending E. coli; chronic pyelonephritis scars the poles and leaves thyroid-like tubules full of casts.'],
s:[['micro','Urinary infection: E. coli most; S. saprophyticus in young women; Proteus, Klebsiella; Enterococcus. Nitrites positive means Gram-negative organisms that reduce nitrate; leukocyte esterase marks white cells. Sterile pyuria suggests renal TB, chlamydia or interstitial nephritis.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'After a long operation complicated by hypotension, creatinine rises; the urine shows muddy brown casts and a FENa of 3%. Diagnosis?',o:['Prerenal azotaemia from ongoing hypovolaemia','Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis','Postrenal obstruction from a blocked catheter','Acute tubular necrosis'],a:3,d:'path',c:'aki',x:'Hypotension starved the tubules: dead epithelial cells slough into muddy brown granular casts, and damaged tubules cannot hold sodium, so FENa rises above 2%. Prerenal injury is the tempting answer after hypotension, but it keeps FENa below 1% with concentrated urine and a bland sediment.'},
{q:'Ten days after starting a penicillin, a patient has fever, rash, blood eosinophilia, sterile pyuria with white cell casts and a rising creatinine. Diagnosis?',o:['Acute tubular necrosis','Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis','Acute interstitial nephritis','Prerenal azotaemia'],a:2,d:'path',c:'aki',x:'The drug acts as a hapten and triggers a type IV (sometimes partly IgE-driven) reaction in the interstitium, typically 1–2 weeks after starting penicillins, NSAIDs, PPIs or diuretics; white cell casts without bacteria and systemic allergy signs are the clues. Stopping the drug (with or without steroids) usually allows recovery, whereas ATN gives muddy brown casts and no rash.'},
{q:'A man with gout has a stone that is invisible on plain X-ray but seen on CT. Composition and prevention?',o:['Uric acid; alkalinise the urine','Calcium oxalate; thiazide','Struvite; antibiotics and removal','Cystine; acidify the urine'],a:0,d:'path',c:'stones',x:'Uric acid stones are radiolucent on plain film and form in persistently acidic urine, as in gout and tumour lysis; potassium citrate raises urine pH so they dissolve, and allopurinol lowers urate. Cystine stones also need alkalinisation, not acidification, so the last option has the wrong treatment.'},
{q:'Adult polycystic kidney disease raises the risk of which brain event?',o:['Lacunar stroke from hypertensive arteriolosclerosis','Low-grade glioma','Subarachnoid haemorrhage','Normal-pressure hydrocephalus'],a:2,d:'path',c:'polycystic-kidney',x:'Polycystin defects weaken vessel walls as well as tubules, so saccular (berry) aneurysms form in the circle of Willis, and rupture causes a sudden worst-ever headache with subarachnoid blood. Hypertension from the disease also raises the risk of lacunar stroke, which makes that option tempting, but the aneurysm is the classic link.'},
{q:'In chronic kidney disease, why does PTH rise?',o:['Low phosphate stimulates the glands','Phosphate retention and low calcitriol lower calcium','High calcitriol stimulates the glands','A parathyroid adenoma forms'],a:1,d:'phys',c:'calcium-pth',x:'Failing kidneys retain phosphate (which binds calcium and raises FGF23) and lose 1α-hydroxylase, so calcitriol falls, gut calcium absorption drops and serum calcium falls; the parathyroids respond with secondary hyperparathyroidism and bone resorption. An adenoma causes primary hyperparathyroidism with high calcium and low phosphate, the opposite picture.'},
{q:'After a crush injury, the urine dipstick is strongly positive for blood but microscopy shows no red cells. Most dangerous early electrolyte change?',o:['Hyponatraemia','Hypercalcaemia','Hypermagnesaemia','Hyperkalaemia'],a:3,d:'path',c:'rhabdomyolysis',x:'Crushed muscle releases myoglobin (which reads as blood on the dipstick but shows no cells), potassium, phosphate and urate; hyperkalaemia can stop the heart within hours, and myoglobin causes tubular necrosis. Calcium falls early, as it binds phosphate and deposits in damaged muscle, and may rebound high only during recovery.'},
{q:'A 60-year-old smoker has haematuria, a flank mass, a haemoglobin of 190 g/L and a new left varicocele that does not empty when he lies down. Diagnosis?',o:['Renal cell carcinoma','Wilms tumour (nephroblastoma)','Angiomyolipoma with tuberous sclerosis','Urothelial carcinoma of the bladder'],a:0,d:'path',x:'Clear cell renal carcinoma (VHL loss, HIF-driven growth) can secrete erythropoietin (polycythaemia) and grows into the renal vein; the left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein, so the blockage causes a varicocele that persists lying down. Angiomyolipoma (tuberous sclerosis) and Wilms tumour (children) do not produce this pattern.'}
]};
